## Where this sits in the field *(optional — fill if the field has an answer; delete if N/A)*
Positions the work so the agent knows what would be genuinely new and what *not* to over-claim.
- **The gap it fills / what's new:** <the specific gap vs prior work — or "engineering reuse, not novelty">
- **Adjacent work / nearest prior results:** <the closest methods or numbers to beat>
- **Threshold ladder & named precedents:** the field's meaningful rungs for the key quantity — the values
  separating *trivial* / *useful* / *state-of-the-art*, each against a named precedent — so every reported
  number is read against them (e.g. SiV fidelity: witness-floor 0.5 · Werner 2/3 · distillation 0.78 ·
  remote-entanglement 0.9 · modern 0.95 · fault-tolerance 0.99, vs Bhaskar 2020 ≈ 0.78 raw). *Build it once,
  cite it every time; skip if the quantity has no canonical thresholds — never invent them.*
- **What NOT to claim:** <over-claims to avoid given the above>

## Loosely-held expectations (hypotheses to test, not commitments)
Held loosely on purpose — these are what the campaign is *testing*, and updating them as evidence arrives
is success, not failure.
- <what we expect / suspect — and how a result would confirm or kill it>
